    {
      "label": "COSC3500 Assignment Specification: Parallel Programming Techniques",
      "file_type": "document",
      "source_file": "COSC3500_Assignment.pdf",
      "source_location": "page 1",
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Implement three matrix multiply functions for three hardware configurations: CPU (AVX/OpenMP, 4 cores), GPU (CUDA, 1 GPU), and a cluster of two nodes (MPI, 2 nodes x 4 cores). Performance is benchmarked relative to a gold-standard reference (Intel MKL for CPU and MPI, CUBLAS for GPU) on NxN matrices; marks depend on how close runtime is to the reference, capped at 7 per implementation (21 total).",
      "id": "cosc3500_assignment_spec",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "cosc3500 assignment specification: parallel programming techniques"
    },
    {
      "label": "Assignment1_GradeBot",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "COSC3500_Assignment.pdf",
      "source_location": "page 2",
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Automated marking binary; usage: ./Assignment1_GradeBot {matrix dimension} {threadCount} {runBenchmarkCPU} {runBenchmarkGPU} {runBenchmarkMPI} {optional integer}... Optional trailing integers are passed through to the matrixMultiply routines as the args parameter (argCount) for debugging/tweaking, but will not exist when the final JudgementDay script runs. Outputs to stdout plus per-node text files. Exists to give students instant feedback with the exact harness used for final grading.",
      "id": "cosc3500_assignment_gradebot",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "assignment1_gradebot"
    },
    {
      "label": "Marking Rubric (Table 1)",
      "file_type": "concept",
      "source_file": "COSC3500_Assignment.pdf",
      "source_location": "page 1",
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "Marks scale with runtime slowdown factor relative to the reference (lower is better). CPU thresholds: 1.0->7, 1.5->6, 3.0->5, 6.0->4, 12.0->3. GPU: 1.5->7, 2.0->6, 3.0->5, 4.0->4, 5.0->3. MPI: 0.6->7, 1.0->6, 1.5->5, 3.0->4, 6.0->3. 2 marks: more than twice as slow as the threshold for 3 but no timeout; 1 mark: compiles and runs but wrong answer; 0: does not compile, not submitted, or timeout. Capped at 7 per implementation, 21 total.",
      "id": "cosc3500_assignment_marking_rubric",
      "community": 2,
      "norm_label": "marking rubric (table 1)"
    },
    {
      "label": "Benchmark Procedure",
      "file_type": "rationale",
      "source_file": "COSC3500_Assignment.pdf",
      "source_location": "page 1",
      "source_url": null,
      "captured_at": null,
      "author": null,
      "contributor": null,
      "rationale": "A set of random unitary square matrices is created and successively multiplied together (a sequence of A = A.B, feeding each result into the next multiply) to get a final solution, which is checked against the reference within an allowable floating point error and timed relative to Intel MKL / CUBLAS. For CPU and GPU the whole multiplication lives in one memory space. For MPI, every node already holds an identical full copy of all input matrices (so no test-data distribution is needed), but each node computes only its own portion of each product, so students must ensure all nodes end each step with the full current product matrix, since it is the input to the next multiplication. Final grading runs CPU and GPU benchmarks once on each of two nodes and takes the fastest (most favourable) node result.",
      "id": "cosc3500_assignment_benchmark_procedure",
      "community": 0,
      "norm_label": "benchmark procedure"
    },
